{"success": true, "name": "arc-stream-review", "file": "references/periodic-row-sequence.md", "content": "# Periodic row-sequence demonstrations

When a demo contains a compact non-background motif and the test contains full-width rows of single colors, consider whether the task is sequence extrapolation across rows rather than a cell-local transformation. Read the input/output row-by-row, identify unchanged rows and inserted/recolored rows, and infer any cycle only after checking the complete output. Apply the resulting sequence to the test's row structure and verify every row, including the cycle boundary; do not assume original test rows are all preserved as markers.

For the reviewed example, the 5x5 input rows were `1,9,7,0,0`; `9,1,9,7,0`; `0,9,1,9,7`; `0,0,9,1,9`; `0,0,0,9,1`. The output continued/filled the row-wise periodic pattern so each row became a repeated sequence of colors, with row starts cycling `1,9,7,9,1`. This is a clue to test row-cycle extrapolation, not sufficient alone to assert a unique rule for a different test.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545217/attempts/771c27e3136742b79bef1ff131c04e1e/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-stream-review/references/periodic-row-sequence.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-stream-review-discipline", "file": "references/post-error-same-premise.md", "content": "# Post-error: do not resubmit the same premise

When a submission is marked incorrect, treat it as evidence that the complete proposed transformation is not sufficiently supported. Reconstruct the demos' changed and unchanged cells, then separate what they establish from what they do not establish about the test's more complex structure. Do not immediately repeat the same grid or make a cosmetic adjustment while retaining the failed premise. Reopen materially different rule families, and request another paid demo only if its possible outcomes discriminate among those families and can change the test prediction. A second submission should rely on new evidence or a genuinely distinct hypothesis that has been replayed against all demonstrations.

In the reviewed instance, simple stripe demonstrations showed selective extension/recoloring behavior but did not warrant applying a guessed global pattern to the test markers. The subsequent submission retained the original unsupported prediction despite the explicit post-error reassessment requirement; this is precisely the avoidable failure mode.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545217/attempts/771c27e3136742b79bef1ff131c04e1e/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-stream-review-discipline/references/post-error-same-premise.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-stream-review", "file": "references/post-incorrect-reassessment.md", "content": "# Reassessment after an incorrect stream submission

An incorrect answer falsifies the complete inferred transformation, not merely a few predicted cells. Before the next action, replay each demonstration from its original input and output, recording all changed cells and meaningful unchanged candidates. Identify what the failed prediction assumed and which demonstration evidence rules that assumption out. Do not reuse the prior output with a few cell edits unless the demos independently establish that local correction.

For the next costly demo, name concrete competing hypotheses and the input feature whose possible forms would cause different outputs. If the hypotheses do not produce different test predictions, a demo has no decision value; if they do, request only when a new pair could distinguish them. Keep the one-action-per-turn contract, and only submit after the reconstructed rule predicts changes and non-changes throughout all available pairs.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545217/attempts/771c27e3136742b79bef1ff131c04e1e/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-stream-review/references/post-incorrect-reassessment.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-evidence-discipline", "file": "references/heterogeneous-shape-cleaning.md", "content": "# Heterogeneous object-cleaning demos: session note

When several differently sized grids contain solid monochrome rectangles among sparse alien-colored marks, don't infer a simple global recoloring from palette overlap. Compare each pair's deltas and classify objects: the demos can show preservation of intact rectangles while isolated noise is erased and intrusions inside a target object are repaired. Record separately (a) isolated marks cleared to the background, (b) interior foreign-color cells replaced by the enclosing object's color, (c) genuine missing cells filled to complete a rectangle, and (d) non-target/background regions left unchanged. Do not automatically fill every connected component's bounding box: validate each proposed target's original geometry and its output mask. The common operation may be shape- or context-selected rather than applying to every colored cell.

After a wrong answer, reject the entire selection/repair hypothesis and replay all demos; changing only the set of speckles or rectangle bounds is not sufficient. A new paid example is worthwhile only when surviving selection rules predict different test outputs. Mechanically generate and validate the output grid before submission.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545217/attempts/771c27e3136742b79bef1ff131c04e1e/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-demo-evidence-discipline/references/heterogeneous-shape-cleaning.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-stream-review", "file": "references/demo-browsing-loop-retrospective.md", "content": "# Retrospective: reference browsing versus evidence analysis

In this instance, multiple paid demos were returned, but the next action was repeatedly preceded by loading additional overlapping skills and reference notes rather than extracting the returned grids' coordinate-level deltas. Each follow-up request therefore spent budget without explicitly testing how new evidence changed candidate rules or the test prediction. The reusable correction is procedural: after loading the relevant class workflow, return to the actual examples; log changed coordinates and meaningful unchanged cells, update complete candidate test outputs, and request again only for a specific live disagreement. Reference lookup is appropriate for a concrete unresolved method question, not as a substitute for analyzing puzzle evidence. This retrospective records a process lesson, not a validated solution to the instance.
